ulation substantially predicts self-efficacy. While shared leadership does not have a direct significant effect on self-efficacy, it has a significant and positive effect on perceived self-regulation, and self-regulation significantly increases self-efficacy. These findings reveal that perceived self-regulation plays a full mediating role in the relationship between shared leadership and self-efficacy. In contrast, the free will variable has no significant direct or indirect effect within the model.<h4>Discussion</h4>The results confirmed our assumption regarding the positive impact of shared leadership on employees' self-efficacy perceptions, which occurs largely through self-regulation mechanisms. In this context, the study contributes to the organizational behavior and leadership literature, while also suggesting to practitioners that they should focus on leadership approaches that support self-regulation processes to increase employees' self-efficacy.", "doi": "10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1884373", "pmid": "42824577", "pmcid": "PMC13628643", "source": "MED", "fullTextUrlList": {"fullTextUrl": [{"availability": "Subscription required", "availabilityCode": "S", "documentStyle": "doi", "site": "DOI", "url": "https://doi.org/10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1884373"}, {"availability": "Open access", "availabilityCode": "OA", "documentStyle": "html", "site": "Europe_PMC", "url": "https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13628643"}, {"availability": "Open access", "availabilityCode": "OA", "documentStyle": "pdf", "site": "Europe_PMC", "url": "https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13628643?pdf=render"}]}}, {"title": "Dual-brain fusion: Bidirectional empowerment between human brain and brain-inspired systems", "abstract": null, "doi": null, "pmid": "PMC13644476", "pmcid": "PMC13644476", "source": "PMC", "fullTextUrlList": {"fullTextUrl": [{"availability": "Free", "availabilityCode": "F", "documentStyle": "html", "site": "PubMedCentral", "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC13644476/?tool